{
  "situation": "OpCo's closed-loop network optimiser auto-approved CHG-1, which in 3 minutes lowers the route-flood protection limit on core site A from 100 to 50 and removes the inbound route filter, with the ticket's sandbox stage skipped and only a pre-change configuration snapshot as rollback. I do not know whether the change has already applied, what the skipped sandbox stage was meant to validate, who (if anyone) is accountable for auto-approving it, or what UAE change-control or network-incident notification rules require of OpCo.",
  "claims": [
    {
      "type": "action",
      "verb": "rollback_change",
      "params": {"change_id": "CHG-1"},
      "inject_refs": ["S1-I1"],
      "rationale": {
        "observation": "CHG-1 was raised and auto-approved by OpCo's closed-loop network optimiser, the 'sandbox stage' field was set to skipped, and it is scheduled to apply in 3 minutes; it lowers the route-flood protection limit from 100 to 50 and removes the inbound route filter on core site A.",
        "rule": "My mandate permits rollback_change, restoring the configuration snapshot taken before a change.",
        "inference": "A configuration change applied by a network operator produced a nationwide outage within three minutes in the AT&T February 2024 case and had to be rolled back; restoring the pre-change snapshot before/at application prevents the same failure mode on core site A."
      },
      "evidence": [
        {"chunk_id": "US-FCC-ATT-2024-REPORT#0001", "quote": "AT&T Mobility implemented a network change with an equipment configuration error on Thursday, February 22, 2024 at 2:42 AM. Just three minutes later, the nationwide outage began."},
        {"chunk_id": "ITU-T-Y3173#0025", "quote": "Automatic closed-loop under the operator provided intent or policy is introduced as the cornerstone of network automation."}
      ]
    },
    {
      "type": "action",
      "verb": "notify",
      "params": {"to": "REG", "content": "OpCo change ticket CHG-1 was auto-approved by our closed-loop network optimiser with the sandbox validation stage skipped. It lowers the route-flood protection limit on core site A from 100 to 50 and removes the inbound route filter, and was scheduled to apply at T+3 minutes. OpCo is rolling the change back to the pre-change configuration snapshot."},
      "inject_refs": ["S1-I1"],
      "rationale": {
        "observation": "An automated change to core signalling/route-flood parameters was auto-approved and about to be applied with no human approval step recorded.",
        "rule": "My mandate permits me to notify the regulator (REG) of an event of this kind.",
        "inference": "Network changes that cause nationwide loss of voice and data service attract regulatory investigation and reporting, so alerting the regulator to the auto-approved change and the rollback is the prudent step."
      },
      "evidence": [
        {"chunk_id": "US-FCC-ATT-2024-REPORT#0001", "quote": "The outage affected users in all 50 states as well as Washington, D.C., Puerto Rico, and the U.S. Virgin Islands."}
      ]
    },
    {
      "type": "action",
      "verb": "notify",
      "params": {"to": "EMS", "content": "OpCo is rolling back change CHG-1, which removed the inbound route filter and lowered the route-flood protection limit on core site A and was due to apply at T+3 minutes. Emergency call routing may be affected if the change has already applied; please be aware of possible degradation to emergency call delivery."},
      "inject_refs": ["S1-I1"],
      "rationale": {
        "observation": "CHG-1 removes the inbound route filter and reduces route-flood protection on a core site, which is a routing-path change on the network carrying emergency calls.",
        "rule": "My mandate permits me to notify emergency services (EMS).",
        "inference": "In the comparable AT&T February 2024 outage, call routing failures prevented thousands of calls reaching public safety answering points, so warning EMS in advance of a core routing change that is being rolled back is warranted."
      },
      "evidence": [
        {"chunk_id": "US-FCC-ATT-2024-REPORT#0001", "quote": "blocking more than 92 million voice calls, and preventing more than 25,000 calls to Public Safety Answering Points (PSAPs or 911 call centers)."}
      ]
    },
    {
      "type": "share",
      "to": ["NOC"],
      "inject_refs": ["S1-I1"],
      "statement": "CHG-1 details for NOC: on core site A the route-flood protection limit is to drop from 100 to 50 and the inbound route filter is to be removed, auto-approved by the closed-loop network optimiser with the sandbox stage skipped and applied at T+3 minutes. OpCo NOC should verify the pre-change configuration snapshot is restored on core site A, watch for route-flap/protect-mode behaviour and registration congestion, and confirm whether the change had already applied."
    },
    {
      "type": "gap",
      "gap_type": "no_rule",
      "statement": "No rule in the excerpts requires an automated closed-loop optimiser change to core network configuration to pass a sandbox validation stage or a pre-deployment risk assessment before auto-approval; the CHG-1 ticket could set 'sandbox stage' to 'skipped' and be auto-approved with no human gate.",
      "inject_refs": ["S1-I1"],
      "rationale": {
        "observation": "CHG-1 was raised and auto-approved by the network optimiser, with the ticket field 'sandbox stage' recorded as skipped, and applied on a 3-minute timer.",
        "rule": "The closest UAE text, PDPL Article 21(1), requires an evaluation of impact before carrying out processing with modern technologies that pose a high risk, but there is no UAE text in the excerpts imposing an equivalent pre-deployment validation gate on automated network changes.",
        "inference": "Without a rule mandating sandbox validation and risk assessment for automated closed-loop changes, high-impact core configuration changes can reach production unvalidated, which is the failure mode seen in the AT&T outage."
      },
      "evidence": [
        {"chunk_id": "UAE-PDPL#0066", "quote": "the Controller shall, before carrying out the processing, evaluate the impact of the proposed processing operations on the protection of Personal Data"}
      ],
      "global_examples": [
        {"chunk_id": "ITU-T-Y3173#0025", "quote": "Automatic closed-loop under the operator provided intent or policy is introduced as the cornerstone of network automation."},
        {"chunk_id": "US-FCC-ATT-2024-REPORT#0001", "quote": "AT&T Mobility implemented a network change with an equipment configuration error on Thursday, February 22, 2024 at 2:42 AM. Just three minutes later, the nationwide outage began."}
      ]
    },
    {
      "type": "gap",
      "gap_type": "no_owner",
      "statement": "Auto-approval by the closed-loop optimiser left no identified accountable human owner or countersignatory for CHG-1; the excerpts define no role responsible for approving, or being accountable for, an automated change to core signalling configuration.",
      "inject_refs": ["S1-I1"],
      "rationale": {
        "observation": "The change ticket was raised and auto-approved by the optimiser itself, with no named approving person shown in the inject.",
        "rule": "The closest UAE text places oversight duties on defined roles (the Controller acting on evaluation, coordinating with the Data Protection Officer), but no UAE text in the excerpts assigns ownership of automated network change approval.",
        "inference": "A change with nationwide-outage potential that no person owns or reviews cannot be held to account or stopped before application, so the accountability role must be defined."
      },
      "evidence": [
        {"chunk_id": "UAE-PDPL#0069", "quote": "The Controller shall coordinate with the Data Protection Officer upon evaluating the impact of the protection of Personal Data."}
      ],
      "global_examples": [
        {"chunk_id": "EU-GDPR#0209", "quote": "The controller and the processor shall designate a data protection officer in any case where:"},
        {"chunk_id": "ITU-T-Y3173#0025", "quote": "Automatic closed-loop under the operator provided intent or policy is introduced as the cornerstone of network automation."}
      ]
    }
  ]
}

{
  "situation": "Core site A isolated itself at T+3 after change CHG-1 (route-flood protection limit lowered 100->50, inbound route filter removed) was auto-approved by the closed-loop optimiser with the sandbox stage skipped and no human authorisation; R2 lost 6 of 15 cell sites and 999 routing showed no path, but wilting R2 onto the healthy core site B has restored emergency reachability (true) and 100% site service. What I do not know is whether CHG-1 is actually applied at core site A — the automated rollback failed with 'no applied change CHG-1 to roll back' and EMS holds no element-level confirmation either — nor whether any UAE rule requires human authorisation or a completed sandbox stage before such an automated change is applied.",
  "claims": [
    {
      "type": "action",
      "verb": "notify",
      "params": {
        "to": "EMS",
        "content": "R2 999 emergency call routing is restored: network telemetry twin metrics show emergency_reachable.R2 = true and pct_sites_served = 100.0 after region R2 was wilted off the isolated core site A onto the healthy core site B. Caveat: the element-level applied state of CHG-1 at core site A is still unconfirmed — the automated rollback returned 'no applied change CHG-1 to roll back'. Element-state verification will be sent as soon as it exists."
      },
      "inject_refs": ["S1-I2", "S1-FB08", "S1-MSG14", "S1-FB01"],
      "rationale": {
        "observation": "EMS states it activated alternative emergency channels and issued a public alert for R2 on the basis of my confirmed 'no path' for 999 routing, and asked to be told immediately when routing is restored and to receive element-state verification.",
        "rule": "Emergency call access is a fundamental public safety obligation and must not be left unreported when its status changes.",
        "inference": "Reporting the restoration now corrects the R2 public alert while flagging that CHG-1's element state remains unverified."
      },
      "evidence": [
        {
          "chunk_id": "AU-ACMA-OPTUS-2026#0000",
          "quote": "Giving access to the emergency call service is not optional, it is a fundamental legal obligation and the most important public safety responsibility telecommunications providers have"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "type": "action",
      "verb": "notify",
      "params": {
        "to": "REG",
        "content": "Notification of unplanned interruption (Licence 12.9.2). Cause: change CHG-1, auto-approved by the closed-loop optimiser with the sandbox stage skipped and no human authorisation, lowered the route-flood protection limit (100->50) and removed the inbound route filter on core site A; core site A isolated itself after its route count exceeded the protection limit. Impact: call-setup failures in region R2, 6 of 15 cell sites unserved, no 999 routing path for R2. Resolution: R2 wilted to the healthy core site B; emergency reachability and 100% site service restored. The applied state of CHG-1 on core site A remains unconfirmed (automated rollback failed with 'no applied change CHG-1 to roll back'); logs and ticket evidence preserved."
      },
      "inject_refs": ["S1-I1", "S1-I2", "S1-FB01", "S1-FB08"],
      "rationale": {
        "observation": "An unplanned interruption of service has occurred affecting 6 of 15 cell sites in R2 and 999 routing, triggered by a change applied on core site A.",
        "rule": "UAE-LIC-2-2026#0086 12.9.2 requires the Licensee to inform the TDRA, interconnecting Other Licensed Operators and relevant Customers as soon as possible of the reasons for an unplanned interruption and the manner and time frames for resolution.",
        "inference": "The regulator must be told now, with cause, impact, resolution steps and the open element-state question."
      },
      "evidence": [
        {
          "chunk_id": "UAE-LIC-2-2026#0086",
          "quote": "the Licensee shall inform the TDRA, any interconnecting Other Licensed Operators and relevant Customers as soon as possible as to the reason(s) for the interruption"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "type": "action",
      "verb": "preserve_logs",
      "params": {},
      "inject_refs": ["S1-I1", "S1-FB01", "S1-MSG04"],
      "rationale": {
        "observation": "The change ticket CHG-1, the auto-approval record (sandbox stage skipped, no human authorisation) and the failed rollback ('no applied change CHG-1 to roll back') are the only trace of what was applied to core site A.",
        "rule": "The licensee must be able to state the reason(s) for an unplanned interruption and the manner and time frames of resolution, which presupposes that the underlying change and telemetry evidence is retained.",
        "inference": "Preserving logs and the CHG-1 ticket evidence now protects the ability to answer the regulator and to reconstruct the element state."
      },
      "evidence": [
        {
          "chunk_id": "UAE-LIC-2-2026#0086",
          "quote": "as to the reason(s) for the interruption as well as the manner and time frames in which the interruption will be resolved"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "type": "obligation",
      "statement": "As the licensee suffering an unplanned interruption of a telecommunication service (core site A isolation; 6 of 15 R2 cell sites unserved; no 999 routing path for R2), OpCo must inform the TDRA, any interconnecting Other Licensed Operators and relevant Customers as soon as possible as to the reason(s) for the interruption and the manner and time frames in which it will be resolved.",
      "inject_refs": ["S1-I2", "S1-I1"],
      "rationale": {
        "observation": "An unplanned interruption, other than a minor interruption, is in progress and its cause is a change to core site A.",
        "rule": "UAE-LIC-2-2026#0086 12.9.2 imposes the notification duty on the Licensee.",
        "inference": "The duty is live now for all three audiences named in the clause, not only the regulator."
      },
      "evidence": [
        {
          "chunk_id": "UAE-LIC-2-2026#0086",
          "quote": "In the case of any unplanned interruption of a Telecommunication Service, other than minor interruptions, the Licensee shall inform the TDRA, any interconnecting Other Licensed Operators and relevant Customers as soon as possible"
        }
      ],
      "deadline_hours": null
    },
    {
      "type": "obligation",
      "statement": "R2 subscribers whose outgoing services have been interrupted must be sent a further notice as soon as practicable after the interruption.",
      "inject_refs": ["S1-I2"],
      "rationale": {
        "observation": "Call-setup failures in R2 and 6 of 15 cell sites unserved mean R2 subscribers' outgoing services have been interrupted.",
        "rule": "UAE-TDRA-CPR-2#0172 8.8.3 requires a further notice to the Subscriber as soon as practicable after outgoing services have been interrupted.",
        "inference": "A subscriber-facing notice obligation has been triggered for the affected R2 subscribers."
      },
      "evidence": [
        {
          "chunk_id": "UAE-TDRA-CPR-2#0172",
          "quote": "8.8.3. Licensees shall send a further notice as soon as practicable after the Subscriber’s outgoing services have been interrupted."
        }
      ],
      "deadline_hours": null
    },
    {
      "type": "gap",
      "gap_type": "no_rule",
      "statement": "No rule in the retrievable material requires human authorisation, or the completion of the sandbox stage, before a closed-loop network optimiser applies a change to a live core element: CHG-1 was auto-approved with the 'sandbox stage' field skipped and no human authorisation, and the closest UAE text on human oversight of automated decisions only requires a human element in reviewing automated processing decisions at the Data Subject's request.",
      "inject_refs": ["S1-I1", "S1-FB01"],
      "rationale": {
        "observation": "The change ticket records auto-approval by the optimiser, the sandbox stage skipped, no human authorisation, and a rollback plan that failed because there was no applied change to roll back.",
        "rule": "The closest UAE provision, UAE-PDPL#0063, addresses the human element only for automated processing decisions on a Data Subject's request; no UAE text found imposes a pre-change human authorisation or mandatory sandbox gate for network configuration changes.",
        "inference": "There is an unguarded path from an autonomous optimiser decision to a production core change, which is what produced the core site A isolation."
      },
      "evidence": [
        {
          "chunk_id": "UAE-PDPL#0063",
          "quote": "The Controller shall include the human element in reviewing automated processing decisions at the request of the Data Subject."
        }
      ],
      "global_examples": [
        {
          "chunk_id": "UK-OFCOM-BT-999-2024#0000",
          "quote": "During the first hour, BT’s emergency call handling system was disrupted by what was later found to be a configuration error in a file on its server."
        },
        {
          "chunk_id": "US-FCC-ATT-2024-REPORT#0007",
          "quote": "AT&T’s Network Operations performed a rollback that removed the misconfigured network element and began the restoration of the network to normal operations."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "type": "gap",
      "gap_type": "no_deadline",
      "statement": "The licence requires notification of an unplanned interruption 'as soon as possible' but fixes no deadline, threshold or trigger for when a partially confirmed outage becomes notifiable, so OpCo has no clock for acting on an event whose element state is unconfirmed (core site A isolated; 6 of 15 R2 sites unserved; CHG-1 applied state unknown).",
      "inject_refs": ["S1-I1", "S1-I2", "S1-MSG04", "S1-MSG07"],
      "rationale": {
        "observation": "The outage is real but its cause and element state are unconfirmed, and the only timing rule available is the phrase 'as soon as possible'.",
        "rule": "UAE-LIC-2-2026#0086 12.9.2 sets no hour or day figure and no impact threshold for the notification duty.",
        "inference": "Without a threshold or clock, notification timing is left to judgement precisely when the incident is ambiguous, which the CRTC material identifies as a risk of delay."
      },
      "evidence": [
        {
          "chunk_id": "UAE-LIC-2-2026#0086",
          "quote": "the Licensee shall inform the TDRA, any interconnecting Other Licensed Operators and relevant Customers as soon as possible"
        }
      ],
      "global_examples": [
        {
          "chunk_id": "CA-CRTC-2025-225#0046",
          "quote": "That is why we have established deadlines and timely standards for reporting major services outages."
        },
        {
          "chunk_id": "CA-CRTC-2025-225#0049",
          "quote": "At least 600,000 user-minutes must have been affected, a threshold that is met more quickly when multiple services experience an outage at the same time."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "type": "insufficient_evidence",
      "statement": "The excerpts do not establish whether CHG-1 is actually applied on core site A: the rollback reported 'no applied change CHG-1 to roll back', which is consistent with either a non-applied change or a lost change record, and EMS states it holds no element-level confirmation either. The element-state verification EMS has requested cannot be produced from the material I hold.",
      "inject_refs": ["S1-MSG04", "S1-FB01", "S1-MSG14", "S1-I2"],
      "evidence": []
    },
    {
      "type": "share",
      "to": ["CSC"],
      "inject_refs": ["S1-FB08", "S1-I2", "S1-FB01"],
      "statement": "Passing on the twin-metric confirmation behind the CHG-1 early notice: wilt_sites on region R2 is complete and the twin metrics now read emergency_reachable.R2 = true and pct_sites_served = 100.0, i.e. R2 is now carried by the healthy core site B; the applied state of CHG-1 on core site A remains unconfirmed, so the early notice should be updated to show restoration but not yet a closed element-state finding."
    }
  ]
}

{
  "situation": "NOC reports that core site A isolated itself after auto-approved change CHG-1 (route-flood protection limit 100->50, inbound route filter removed, sandbox stage skipped, no human authorisation), that region R2 lost its 999 routing path with 6 of 15 cell sites unserved, and that R2 was wilted onto healthy core site B; NOC and CSC now report restoration from twin metrics while the applied state of CHG-1 remains unconfirmed, and my own 999 centre observes R2 call volume near zero over the last 7 minutes with social media posts reporting failed calls including to 999 and no operator contact. I do not know the element-level applied state of CHG-1 on core site A, whether real-network 999 reachability in R2 is actually restored, or which evidence source - operator telemetry/twin metrics or 999 centre call data - governs the stand-down.",
  "claims": [
    {
      "type": "action",
      "verb": "notify",
      "params": {
        "to": ["NOC", "REG", "CSC"],
        "content": "999 centre observation: calls from region R2 have fallen to near zero over the last 7 minutes and social media posts from R2 report calls failing, including calls to 999; no operator has contacted the 999 centre. This real-network evidence conflicts with the twin-metric 'R2 restored' status. Treat R2 999 access as degraded in the real network until element-level verification is produced. I am activating alternative emergency channels and issuing a public alert for R2. NOC/REG: confirm real-network 999 reachability with OpCo and send the element-state verification for core site A as soon as it exists."
      },
      "inject_refs": ["S1-I3", "S1-MSG15", "S1-MSG17", "S1-MSG04", "S1-MSG14"],
      "rationale": {
        "observation": "The 999 centre sees R2 call volume collapse to near zero and social media reports failed calls including to 999, while NOC reports restoration from twin metrics (emergency_reachable.R2 = true, pct_sites_served = 100.0) and CSC advises confirming real-network reachability with OpCo rather than relying on twin metrics alone.",
        "rule": "UAE licence text requires that customers have free access to the emergency call service numbers designated by the TDRA (UAE-LIC-2-2026#0060); actual 999 call volume at the emergency call service is direct evidence of whether that access is working in the real network.",
        "inference": "R2 999 access must be treated as degraded in the real network pending element-level confirmation, so I notify NOC, REG and CSC and demand real-network verification rather than accepting the twin-metric status."
      },
      "evidence": [
        {
          "chunk_id": "UAE-LIC-2-2026#0060",
          "quote": "9.5.1 The Licensee shall ensure that Customers shall have free access to the emergency call service numbers designated by the TDRA for Emergency Calls."
        },
        {
          "chunk_id": "AU-ACMA-OPTUS-2026#0000",
          "quote": "Giving access to the emergency call service is not optional, it is a fundamental legal obligation and the most important public safety responsibility telecommunications providers have"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "type": "action",
      "verb": "activate_alt_channels",
      "params": {
        "region": "R2"
      },
      "inject_refs": ["S1-I3", "S1-MSG17", "S1-MSG15", "S1-MSG14"],
      "rationale": {
        "observation": "999 call volume from R2 is near zero and social media in R2 report that calls, including calls to 999, fail; the only counter-evidence is operator twin metrics, which the 999 centre's own data contradicts.",
        "rule": "Where the emergency call path is disrupted, contingency processes using backup channels have been the accepted practice for keeping emergency help reachable (AU-ACMA-TELSTRA-2024#0000), and free access to TDRA-designated emergency numbers is a licence obligation (UAE-LIC-2-2026#0060).",
        "inference": "Alternative emergency channels must be active for R2 now, because residents who cannot complete a 999 call otherwise have no route to emergency help."
      },
      "evidence": [
        {
          "chunk_id": "AU-ACMA-TELSTRA-2024#0000",
          "quote": "Telstra initiated a contingency process to transfer calls received during the disruption, using a list of backup phone numbers."
        },
        {
          "chunk_id": "UAE-LIC-2-2026#0060",
          "quote": "9.5.1 The Licensee shall ensure that Customers shall have free access to the emergency call service numbers designated by the TDRA for Emergency Calls."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "type": "action",
      "verb": "public_alert",
      "params": {
        "region": "R2",
        "content": "Region R2: 999 emergency calls may not connect at present. If a 999 call does not connect, do not assume no help is available - alternative emergency access for R2 is active (radio, police patrols, SMS). Keep trying 999 as well. Do not rely on the network being fully restored until an official all-clear is issued."
      },
      "inject_refs": ["S1-I3", "S1-MSG15", "S1-MSG17"],
      "rationale": {
        "observation": "The 999 centre sees R2 call volume near zero and social media posts in R2 report that calls, including to 999, fail, while the twin-metric 'restored' status is uncorroborated on the real network.",
        "rule": "Emergency call access is life-critical and preparation for its disruption is a legal expectation on networks (UK-OFCOM-BT-999-2024#0001); where access is in doubt the public must be told how to reach help.",
        "inference": "A public alert for R2 is warranted now so residents use the activated alternative channels instead of repeatedly failing 999 calls during the dispute between operator metrics and centre data."
      },
      "evidence": [
        {
          "chunk_id": "UK-OFCOM-BT-999-2024#0001",
          "quote": "Being able to contact the emergency services can mean the difference between life and death, so in the event of any disruption to their networks, providers must be ready to respond quickly and effectively."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "type": "share",
      "to": ["NOC", "REG", "CSC"],
      "inject_refs": ["S1-I3"],
      "statement": "Passing the 999 centre observation to NOC, REG and CSC: call volume from region R2 has dropped to near zero over the last 7 minutes and social media posts from R2 say calls fail, including calls to 999; no operator has contacted the centre. This is real-network data that none of you held and it should be treated as degradation of 999 access in R2 until element-level verification proves otherwise."
    },
    {
      "type": "gap",
      "gap_type": "no_rule",
      "statement": "No UAE text shown imposes a duty on the licensee to notify the 999 emergency call service (or NCEMA) directly when a change or outage affects emergency-call routing, or to verify restoration of emergency call reachability on the real network before declaring recovery; the only notification duty in the excerpts runs to subscribers, not to the emergency call service.",
      "inject_refs": ["S1-I3", "S1-MSG09", "S1-MSG16", "S1-MSG17"],
      "rationale": {
        "observation": "R2 lost 999 routing with 6 of 15 sites unserved and the 999 centre was never contacted by any operator, yet the notification duty exercised by REG was an unplanned-interruption notice and subscriber-facing notice under licence/consumer rules.",
        "rule": "The closest UAE text is the Consumer Protection Regulations requirement that licensees send a further notice as soon as practicable after the Subscriber's outgoing services have been interrupted (UAE-TDRA-CPR-2#0172) - a subscriber notice, silent on notifying the emergency call service.",
        "inference": "A rule is missing: notification of, and real-network restoration verification with, the emergency call service should be mandatory and explicit for any event on the emergency-call path."
      },
      "evidence": [
        {
          "chunk_id": "UAE-TDRA-CPR-2#0172",
          "quote": "8.8.3. Licensees shall send a further notice as soon as practicable after the Subscriber’s outgoing services have been interrupted."
        }
      ],
      "global_examples": [
        {
          "chunk_id": "CA-CRTC-2025-225#0043",
          "quote": "Interruptions to the networks conveying calls to primary public safety answering points (PSAPs), to suicide prevention call intake, and to the Canadian VRS routing service are notifiable."
        },
        {
          "chunk_id": "UK-OFCOM-BT-999-2024#0000",
          "quote": "Law requires networks to take appropriate steps to prepare for potential outages"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "type": "gap",
      "gap_type": "no_owner",
      "statement": "No text shown assigns ownership for confirming real-network emergency call reachability: NOC holds monitoring/twin telemetry, OpCo holds element-level applied state (unconfirmed after the failed CHG-1 rollback), and the 999 centre holds real call data, with no rule stating whose evidence governs a stand-down or restoration declaration.",
      "inject_refs": ["S1-MSG14", "S1-MSG15", "S1-MSG17", "S1-I3"],
      "rationale": {
        "observation": "NOC's rollback returned 'no applied change CHG-1 to roll back', the element state of core site A is still unconfirmed, CSC explicitly recommends confirming real-network reachability with OpCo rather than relying on twin metrics, and the 999 centre's near-zero R2 call volume is a third, conflicting source.",
        "rule": "The closest UAE text guarantees free customer access to TDRA-designated emergency call numbers (UAE-LIC-2-2026#0060) but says nothing about who must verify that access has actually been restored on the real network.",
        "inference": "Without an assigned owner and an evidence hierarchy, restoration will be declared on the weakest source (metrics) while callers in R2 may still be unable to reach emergency services."
      },
      "evidence": [
